@[systemd-boot page]
systemd-boot is a small UEFI boot menu; it comes with systemd (after
@[enchantment install init systemd]). It is UEFI only, and it only reads the
EFI system partition (FAT): the kernel must be there. The simplest layout is
the EFI partition mounted at /boot (the disk-mount step mentions it, for
Limine; about 15 MiB per kernel, 512 MiB to 1 GiB is plenty). Tested in
QEMU (UEFI) with the ESP at /boot/efi and the kernel copied onto it.

bootctl install puts its firmware entry ("Linux Boot Manager") first in the
boot order, and replaces EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI (where grub-install --removable
put GRUB). It also warns when the ESP is readable by everyone: mount it with
umask=0077 in /etc/fstab (e.g. @[vfat umask=0077 0 2]).

In @[enchantment chroot]:
 @[bootctl install]            # copies systemd-boot, adds a firmware entry
 @[blkid -s PARTUUID -o value /dev/sda2]   # your root partition's PARTUUID
Copy the kernel onto the ESP if it isn't there (ESP at /boot/efi:
@[cp /boot/vmlinuz-6.18.59 /boot/efi/]), then write ESP/loader/entries/
smgl.conf (your kernel version and PARTUUID):
  title   Source Mage
  linux   /vmlinuz-6.18.59
  options root=PARTUUID=PUT-IT-HERE rw
and ESP/loader/loader.conf:
  default smgl.conf
  timeout 3
 @[bootctl list]               # checks the entry
 @[exit]

@[enchantment install init systemd] switches off systemd's gpt-auto
generator, which would otherwise mount the ESP the machine booted from on
/efi as well, when /boot is in use (if bootctl names /efi, the system was
switched without it: remove /efi after
 @[ln -s /dev/null /etc/systemd/system-generators/systemd-gpt-auto-generator]
and a reboot).

New kernels need their own entry (or edit this one). @[bootctl update]
updates systemd-boot itself after systemd is recast.
